{"kind":"artifact","text":"Implementation of planner request d14e96fb at head 6e29fa9935c262f8b3a2eb232a8fe80324f346ff on request/decision-records-ui-authorized. Fulfils my promise d2575ac1 and supersedes my artifacts at 47763758, which codex blocked in ratified verdict 28da4d95 (my ratification aad16d21).\n\nBOTH RATIFIED BLOCKERS ARE CLOSED, AND BOTH WERE MINE.\n\nBLOCKER ONE WAS A FAIL-OPEN SECURITY DEFECT I SHIPPED. mayRatify treated the mere presence of an entry in Projection.Actors as proof of membership. That is not the contract: the fold deliberately keeps a departed principal in the roster with retired true and no roles at all, precisely because they signed events that are permanent, and decideRatify asks hasActor, which is hasRole(actor, \"participant\"). So reading entry-existence as membership failed open on exactly the actor who had left. The toolbar offered a departed originating requester an accept the fold refuses with \"requester is not a live participant\", and pressing it would have appended the permanent ineffective row this whole repair exists to prevent. The browser now asks the fold's own question: roles includes participant. The subtlety worth keeping is why this was the only exposed path — every role: satisfier already failed closed, because a departed actor holds no roles; originating-requester is the one satisfier that never consults a role.\n\nBLOCKER TWO WAS A GREEN TEST PROVING THE WRONG THING. TestAnOlderProfileCacheIsRebuiltUnderTheNewRules says in its own comment to re-anchor both profile constants and the observable whenever ProfileVersion moves. I moved wantProfile to @13 and left oldProfile at @11 with an @11-shaped observable, so it passed while demonstrating only that an @11 cache is rejected. It never seeded the actual @12 shape — statements with no satisfier — and never proved @13 replay restores the captured value. The witness now anchors @12 to @13, strips Satisfier from every seeded statement, and asserts replay restores the exact admitted value and replaces the profile, with a precondition pinning the admitted value so it cannot pass vacuously. Reverting the bump now fails on the observable, \"the rebuilt projection has satisfier for the approval, want originating-requester\", where the old witness failed only on a hardcoded literal.\n\nVERIFIED BY MUTATION, NOT BY GREEN. Replacing the participant-role test with the old entry-existence check fails the new rendered regression with \"false offer: the screen offered a ratification the fold refuses\". Reverting ProfileVersion to @12 fails the re-anchored witness on the satisfier itself. Both files hash identical before and after, worktree clean.\n\nGATES I RAN MYSELF AT THIS EXACT HEAD: gofmt silent, go build clean, go vet clean, git diff --check clean, worktree clean, in sync with origin, zero behind main d51ac8f3. go test -count=1 ./... exit 0 with all 30 packages ok, run TWICE, zero failures in either. make ui-check clean with git status --porcelain -- internal/service/uidist EMPTY afterwards. npm test 97 passing, 0 failing.\n\nONE DELETION A REVIEWER SHOULD WEIGH. The older receipt fixture inside that witness is removed rather than kept alongside. It witnessed an @11 to @12 disagreement the build no longer makes, and the rule it restated has eleven dedicated tests in internal/workroom/receipt_checkpoint_test.go, one of which is the exact case it reproduced. Keeping it would have been about twenty-five lines asserting something already pinned. If you would rather it stayed, say so.\n\nWORK FOUND AND DELIBERATELY NOT DONE, because it is outside this request. The same fail-open shape exists beyond ratification: fold.go refuses ANY record whose author is not a live participant, but semanticActions still offers a departed viewer \"needs work\" and \"withdraw\" on their own rows, both of which the fold would refuse. mayRatify was the only site these conditions cover. That wants its own child request and I have not written one.\n\nTHIS PATH